  "slug": "recover-binary-search-tree",
  "title": "Recover Binary Search Tree",
  "difficulty": "Hard",
  "category": "Tree dfs",
  "patterns": [
    "tree",
    "dfs",
    "recursion",
    "bst"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "a valid BST's inorder traversal is strictly increasing - so if exactly two values are swapped, what would you notice while walking the inorder sequence and comparing each value to the one before it?",
    "walk the tree inorder while remembering the previous value visited. whenever the current value is smaller than the previous one, that's a 'violation' - how many violations can two swapped values create, and are they adjacent in the sequence or not?",
    "if the two swapped nodes are adjacent in the inorder sequence, you'll see exactly one violation, and the two nodes involved are the previous and current node at that point. if they're far apart, you'll see two violations - the first node of the first violation and the second node of the second violation are the ones to swap",
    "track two node references (first and second) during the inorder walk: on the first violation set first to the previous node and second to the current node; on a second violation, only update second (to the current node this time). after the traversal, swap first's and second's values"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "assuming the two swapped nodes are always adjacent in the inorder sequence, which only handles the case where one violation appears (fails when the swapped nodes are far apart, producing two violations)",
    "swapping array positions directly instead of swapping the two node values while keeping the tree's shape intact",
    "using the sort-and-compare brute force but forgetting that the output must still be in the original heap-index shape, not just a sorted list of values"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "Binary trees are encoded as flat arrays using complete-binary-tree (heap) indexing:\n\n- the root sits at index 0\n- a node at index `i` has its left child at index `2*i+1` and right child at index `2*i+2`\n- a child index only counts as real if it's within the array's bounds **and** its value isn't the sentinel `-101`\n- `-101` means \"no node there\" - real node values always fall between -100 and 100, so -101 never collides with a genuine value\n\nFor example, `[1, 2, 3, -101, 4]` encodes this tree:\n\n```\n      1\n     / \\\n    2   3\n     \\\n      4\n```\n\nNode 2's left child is missing (sentinel) but its right child is 4.\n\nThe given tree was meant to be a valid binary search tree (BST), but the\nvalues of exactly two of its nodes were accidentally swapped, breaking\nthe BST property. The tree's shape (which positions have nodes at all)\nis correct and untouched, only two values are in the wrong spots.\n\n**Fix it by swapping those two values back, and return the corrected tree in the same heap-index array format.**\n\nUse the same trimming rule as before: fill internal gaps with -101, and trim trailing sentinel positions off the very end of the array.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "2 <= number of real nodes <= 1000",
      "every real node value is between -100 and 100 inclusive",
      "the sentinel value -101 marks a missing node and never appears as a real value",
      "exactly two node values were swapped from an originally valid BST; the tree's shape is unaffected"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "the two swapped nodes are adjacent (parent and child)",
      "the two swapped nodes are far apart with several nodes between them in inorder order",
      "a minimal two-node tree"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [1, 3, -101, -101, 2]",
        "output": "[3, 1, -101, -101, 2]",
        "expectedOutput": "[3, 1, -101, -101, 2]",
        "explanation": "root 1 has left child 3, whose right child is 2. this is invalid because 3 (in the left subtree) should be less than the root. swapping the root and its left child (1 and 3) fixes it: the corrected tree has root 3, left child 1, and 1's right child 2, whose inorder traversal is now the sorted sequence 1, 2, 3"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [3, 1, 4, -101, -101, 2]",
        "output": "[2, 1, 4, -101, -101, 3]",
        "expectedOutput": "[2, 1, 4, -101, -101, 3]",
        "explanation": "root 3 has left child 1 and right child 4, whose left child is 2. this is invalid because 2 (in the right subtree of 3) should be greater than 3. swapping the root and the deeply nested 2 fixes it: the corrected tree's inorder traversal becomes 1, 2, 3, 4"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [1, 2]",
        "output": "[2, 1]",
        "expectedOutput": "[2, 1]",
        "explanation": "root 1 has left child 2, which should be smaller than the root, not larger. swapping the two values gives root 2 with left child 1, a valid two-node BST"
      }
    ],
